          "description": "Compute an effect size and report it WITH A 95% CONFIDENCE INTERVAL. Takes the six group statistics (or a d directly) and returns Cohen's d, Hedges' g and an estimated CI. Reporting the value IS the deliverable. An effect far outside the usual range is informative and is NOT evidence of misconduct, and severity is capped at `suspicious` because an effect size can be improbable but never arithmetically impossible. `field` is OPTIONAL and usually omitted -- magnitude is REPORTED, not judged, because whether an effect is unrealistically large is a judgement for the reader with the paper's effects laid out as a set. PASS `reported_d` ALONGSIDE the group statistics to check INTERNAL CONSISTENCY instead: does the effect size the paper printed match the one its own means, SDs and ns imply? That needs no comparison class and is what catches a mistyped effect size. Supplying `field` additionally places the value against field benchmarks, which are mostly uncited estimates and say so. THIS IS THE CHECK THAT WORKS ON FIGURE-DERIVED DATA: gross implausibility survives digitization error (verified -- the verdict is unchanged across the full +/-37% dispersion error range), so a paper reporting its outcome ONLY as a bar chart becomes checkable here even though GRIM/GRIMMER/SPRITE cannot touch it. Pass provenance='figure_pixels' when the inputs were read off a chart. Before treating a large effect as a finding, rule out the three ordinary causes: dispersion reported as SEM and read as SD (inflates d by sqrt(n)), a waitlist rather than active comparator, and a units mismatch. Parameters: d (optional), field, mean1, sd1, n1, mean2, sd2, n2, design, provenance",
