          "tool_name": "forensic_metascience__check_variance_dispersion",
          "description": "Are these standard deviations too SIMILAR to each other? Sample variances are themselves random, and fabricators who add noise to the means forget that the DISPERSIONS need their own noise. Standardises each variance by the within mean square (z2 = s2/MS_w) and compares max_z - min_z against a parametric bootstrap of the chi-square null; one-sided, for excessive consistency. Hartgerink, Voelkel, Wicherts & van Assen (2019) measured this as the single best fabrication detector tested (AUROC 1.00 on raw data), against 0.38-0.51 for terminal digits and 0.02-0.16 for Benford -- which is why this toolkit has no digit-preference check. Pass `sds`, `ns`, and the SDs AS PRINTED in `sd_texts` (e.g. ['2.11','2.08']): coarse rounding collapses honest SDs onto identical values -- at SD~2.0 printed to 0dp, 60% of HONEST samples give six identical SDs -- so without the printed precision a rounding artifact is indistinguishable from the signature. `homogeneous_variances` is REQUIRED and gates the reassuring verdict too: the source paper's own preregistered analysis scored AUROC 0.264 (inverted, worse than chance) from getting it wrong, and a violated assumption makes this test BLIND rather than accusatory, so an unstated assumption cannot return `consistent` either. Needs >=3 groups that are comparable units on ONE instrument; cluster/multi-site/stratified/crossover/matched designs constrain variances by construction and are refused. SEVERITY IS CAPPED AT `suspicious` AND EVERY RESULT SETS `reference_required`: the paper's AUROC 1.00 was RELATIVE -- every dataset it saw, genuine and fabricated alike, looked significant against the theoretical null, and only comparison against known-genuine data separated them. This ranks candidates for human review; it never convicts. Parameters: sds (list), ns (list), sd_texts (list of the printed literals), homogeneous_variances (required), design, iterations (default 100000), seed (default 0)",
